      "html": "<p>Charax michaeli reaches about 20 cm in length according to the available publications.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/charax-cf-michaeli-2/",
      "html": "<p>Charax michaeli is widespread in the Amazonian drainage of Brazil, Colombia, Peru and probably also Ecuador. In 2022, 9 Charax species from Colombia had been described.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/charax-cf-michaeli-2/",
      "html": "<p>The animals are of a nice orange coloration, slightly transparent like glass. Humpback tetras comprise three genera: Charax (18 species), Cynopotamus (12 species) and Roeboides (22 species), distinguished mainly by their teeth. Cynopotamus are the most specialized fish eaters with long, tusk-like teeth; Charax probably eat mostly small fish in nature and have tusk-like but much shorter teeth; Roeboides appear to be small animal and scale eaters with small teeth suitable for rasping.</p>"
    }
  ],
